  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Friday) \nIssued at 1028 PM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nThe extended forecast package remains on track, with little to no \nchanges made as the main theme is that is going to be mostly hot and \ndry. Through the weekend into early next week, we will continue to \nsee the upper level ridge and associated area of high pressure \ntranslate east, wile flattening, in response to the upper level \ntrough translating through the Intermountain West. Geopotential \nheights will be stagnant, slightly above seasonal standards, while \nthickness values increase subtly each day through the extended. \nDespite increase thickness values, the upper high will remain on the \nweaker side (~594 dam which is still impressive), which should help \nkeep afternoon temperatures from becoming excessively hot. The \nwarmest temperatures are expected to develop off the Caprock, and \nalthough we remain below criteria, if we see any signs of \nstrengthening of the upper ridge, an Advisory may be warranted. \nThough for now, temperatures should remain below the 105 degree \nthreshold, with highs cross the Caprock in the mid to upper 90s, \nwhile off the Caprock temperatures range from the upper 90s to \nlower triple digits. As far as precipitation goes, there is none in \nsight. Persistent subsidence beneath the ridge will continue to \nlimit convective potential through the extended, with dry and quiet \nconditions expected. \n\nThe extended forecast package remains on track, with little to no \nchanges made as the main theme is that its going be mostly hot and \ndry. Through the weekend into early next week we will continue to \nsee the upper level ridge and associated area of high pressure \ntranslate east, while flattening, in response to the upper level \ntrough translating through the Intermountain West. Geopotential \nheights will be stagnant, slightly above seasonal standards, while \nthickness values increase subtly each day through the extended. \nDespite increasing thickness values, the upper high will remain on \nthe weaker side, which should help keep afternoon temperatures from \nbecoming excessively hot. Warmest temperatures are expected to \ndevelop off the Caprock and although we remain below criteria, if we \nsee any signs of strengthening of the upper ridge, an Advisory may \nbe warranted. Though for now, temperatures should remain the 105 \ndegrees threshold with highs across the Caprock in the mid to upper \n90s. While off the Caprock temperatures range from the upper 90s to \nlower triple digits. Persistent subsidence beneath the ridge will \ncontinue to limit convective potential through the extended, with \ndry and quiet conditions expected through much of next week.",
